  "raw_label": "1",
  "texts": {
    "preamble": "Ramesh vs K.S.R.T.C on 20 April, 2024 Kabc0B0018752022 Before The Mact & Xv Addl., Judge, Court Of Small Causes, (Scch-19) Mayo Hall Unit, Bengaluru. Dated this the 20th day of April 2024 Present: Smt. Rajeshwari-J-Puranik, B.A.Ll.B. (Spl.) Xv Addl. Small Causes Judge & Xxiii Acmm, Bengaluru MVC.No.7057/2022 Petitioner: Mr. Ramesh, S/o Nataraj, Aged 41 years, R/at No.266, Bannerghatta Main road, Opp. Food Cooperation of India, Jayanagar, Bangalore-560 069. (By Pleader Sri. Ashok Kumar) -V/s- Respondents: 1. The Managing Director, Ksrtc Central Offices, K.H. Road, Shanthinagar, Bangalore - 560027.",
    "facts": "Brief facts of Petitioner's case, are as under: On 03.10.2022 at about 01.45 a.m, (early morning) the Petitioner was traveling in KSRTC bus bearing Reg.No.KA-57/F-3773, on NH-75 Road, Kolar - Bangalore road, towards Hoskote, when the bus reached near Mylapura Gate, Hosakote Taluk, Bangalore Rural District, driver of said bus drove the same in a rash and negligent manner so as to endangering to human life at high speed and lost control over the bus and dashed to one Lorry bearing Reg.No.KA-16/D-9498, which was parked on left side of the road without any signals or any indication. As a result of forced impact, the Petitioner sustained grievous injuries. 3. Immediately after the accident, the Petitioner was shifted to MVJ Hospital, Hosakote for first aid, then he was shifted to Jayanagar Orthopaedic Center Bengaluru wherein, he took treatment as inpatient and undergone surgery and spent Rs.1,50,000/- towards medical expenses, transportation, conveyance and other charges. 4. It is contended that, this accident occurred due to rash and negligent driving of the driver of the KSRTC Bus and also due to negligent act of 4 SCCH - 19 MVC.7057/2022 the driver of the Lorry. Hence, Hosakote Police registered a case against driver of the KSRTC bus bearing Ramesh vs K.S.R.T.C on 20 April, 2024 Indian Kanoon - http://indiankanoon.org/doc/199618399/ 2 Reg.No.KA-57/F-3773 in crime No.353/2022. 5. It is contended that, prior to the accident the Petitioner was hale, healthy and he was Painter by profession, and earning Rs.25,000/- per month and due to accidental injuries he is not able to do his job and hence he lost his earning and suffering from mental shock and agony. 6. In pursuance to the notice, Respondent No.1 to 4 appeared through their respective Counsels and filed written statement separately. 7. Brief averments of the Written statement of Respondent No.1 are as under: While admitting the ownership of KSRTC bus bearing Reg.No.KA-57/F-3773, this Respondent contended that, this accident occurred not due to rash and negligent driving of the driver of KSRTC Bus, but due to sole negligence on the part of the driver of Lorry bearing Reg.No.KA-16/D-9498 as he was parked the Lorry without any precautionary measurement and signals. Hence, Respondent no.2 & 3 are liable to pay the 5 SCCH - 19 MVC.7057/2022 compensation to the Petitioners. However, on humanitarian ground this Respondent had paid exgratia amount of Rs.11,356/- and same may be liable to be deducted and in case of holding contributory negligence on the part of the Lorry either fully or partially. The insurer and owner of the lorry shall be directed to refund the medical expenses paid by this respondent to the Petitioner to the extent of their liability. 8. Brief averments of the Written statement of Respondent No.2 are as under: The petition is not maintainable either in law or on facts of the case and denied the entire averments of the petition. While admitting about issuance of insurance policy in respect of Lorry bearing Reg.No.KA-16/D-9498 in favour of Mr.Srinivasa, this Respondent restricted its liability to the terms and conditions of the policy. Further contended that, driver of the said vehicle did not possess valid D.L. FC and permit as on the date of the accident. hence, terms and conditions of insurance policy were violated. It is contended that, as per the petition averments this accident occurred due to rash and negligent driving of the 6 SCCH - 19 MVC.7057/2022 driver of KSRTC bus bearing Reg.No.KA-57/F- 3773. 9. Brief averments of the Written statement of Respondent No.3 & 4 are as under: The petition is not maintainable either in law or on facts of the case and denied the entire averments of the petition. While admitting the ownership & driver of Lorry bearing Reg.No.KA- 16/D-9498 these Respondents contended that, this accident occurred due to sole rash and negligent driving of the driver of KSRTC bus bearing Reg.No.KA-57/F-3773. It is contended that, they took all the safety methods with due Ramesh vs K.S.R.T.C on 20 April, 2024 Indian Kanoon - http://indiankanoon.org/doc/199618399/ 3 diligence, because of the rash and negligent driving of driver of respondent No.1 this accident was happened. Respondent No.3 vehicle (Lorry) bearing No.KA-16/D-9498 was breakdown around 1 \"o\" clock , so lorry driver parked the vehicle left side of the road on the footpath. He took the safety precautions about the vehicles parking and placed the stones and camphire near the accident place. The accident occurred due to negligent driving of the KSRTC Bus driver only. Hence the Respondent no.3 and 4 not liable to pay any compensation to the Petitioner. 7 SCCH - 19 MVC.7057/2022 Further Respondents denied the age, income and occupation of the petitioner, medical expenses incurred by him and disability caused to him and also contended that, claim of the petitioner is excessive and exorbitant. Hence , prays to dismiss the petition with costs. 8 SCCH - 19 MVC.7057/2022 11. In order to substantiate the case, Petitioner got examined himself as PW.1 and got examined two witnesses as Pw.2 and 3. They got marked 15 documents as Ex.P1 to Ex.P15. The Driver of KSRTC bus got examined as RW-1 and not got marked any document on his behalf. The Manager of 2nd Respondent Insurance company got examined as Rw.2 and got marked two documents as Ex.R1 & 2. 12.",
    "arguments": "22 . In the petition for compensation U/Sec.166 of Motor Vehicles Act.1988 the claimant has to prove the incident only on the preponderance of the probabilities and standard of proof beyond all reasonable doubt is not required as held by the Hon'ble APEX Court in the decision reported in 2011 SAR (Civil) 319 Kausum and others V/s Satbir and others. Reference in this connection may be made to the decision of this Court in Bimla Devi and others V/s Himachal Road Transport Corporation and others (2009) 13 SCC 530, in which the relevant observation on this point has been made and which is very pertinent and is quoted below:- \"In a situation of this nature, the Tribunal has rightly taken a holistic view of the matter. , in MFA.No.103557/2016 (between Sriram General Insurance Co. ltd., V/s Smt. Lakshmi & others, dd 20-03-2018) the Hon'ble High Court of Karnataka, held that as per Sec.34 of CPC the rate of interest can be awarded in MVC Cases, cannot be more than 6% p.a.",
    "petitioner_arguments": "",
    "respondent_arguments": "",
    "other_lawyer_arguments": "22 . In the petition for compensation U/Sec.166 of Motor Vehicles Act.1988 the claimant has to prove the incident only on the preponderance of the probabilities and standard of proof beyond all reasonable doubt is not required as held by the Hon'ble APEX Court in the decision reported in 2011 SAR (Civil) 319 Kausum and others V/s Satbir and others. Reference in this connection may be made to the decision of this Court in Bimla Devi and others V/s Himachal Road Transport Corporation and others (2009) 13 SCC 530, in which the relevant observation on this point has been made and which is very pertinent and is quoted below:- \"In a situation of this nature, the Tribunal has rightly taken a holistic view of the matter. , in MFA.No.103557/2016 (between Sriram General Insurance Co. ltd., V/s Smt. Lakshmi & others, dd 20-03-2018) the Hon'ble High Court of Karnataka, held that as per Sec.34 of CPC the rate of interest can be awarded in MVC Cases, cannot be more than 6% p.a."
  },
